  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "**Option-1 result (cell 27).** Dividing every raw Q by its most divergent coefficient (the `u^0` coefficient, for every direct and dual Q of both non-singlets) does **not** turn the non-singlet blocks into products:\n\n| state | power | fitted exponents | max log residual |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `{1,1,1,1}` (control) | 8 | 2, 1, 0, 2, 1, 2 (unchanged) | ~10⁻⁷ |\n| `{2,1,1,0}` | 20 | 3.52, 3.51, 2.55, 3.01, 4.58, 3.16 | 3.0 |\n| `{2,2,0,0}` | 20 | 2.61, 0.97, 2.27, 7.06, 4.28, 2.07 | 1.5 |\n\nThe control is unchanged, so the rule behaves. So the divergence of the raw Q's is not the whole story: for non-singlets the leading coefficient is a genuine function of the twists, not one monomial.\n\n**Next test, using existing data.** In the raw normalisation, `C_6({2,1,1,0})` has degree 2 in the twists. If it is a polynomial, it is a quadratic form in `(a_1, a_2, a_3)` with 6 coefficients, fitted here to the 10 rays of cell 25.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* ---- 17. Is C6({2,1,1,0}) (raw normalisation, degree 2) a quadratic polynomial in the twist directions? ---- *)\nClearAll[quadFit];\nquadFit = Module[{data = C6data[[2, 2]], mons, X, y, sol},\n   mons[r_] := {r[[1]]^2, r[[2]]^2, r[[3]]^2, r[[1]] r[[2]], r[[1]] r[[3]], r[[2]] r[[3]]};\n   X = N[mons[#[[1]]] & /@ data, 30]; y = N[data[[All, 3]], 30];\n   sol = LeastSquares[X, y];\n   <|\"state\" -> C6data[[2, 1]], \"powers\" -> Union[data[[All, 2]]], \"coefficients of (a1^2, a2^2, a3^2, a1a2, a1a3, a2a3)\" -> N[sol, 8],\n     \"max relative residual\" -> N[Max[Abs[(X . sol - y)/y]], 3]|>];\nGrid[List @@@ Normal[quadFit], Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left]",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
    {
     "items": [
      {
